{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,320p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1692361792/1/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"87ab05b8\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nErase all distractor pixels and expand the single red control pixel into the grid quadrant that contains it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Exactly one red pixel is the control pixel.\n- Other non-background pixels are distractors.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background color.\n- Split the grid into equal top/bottom and left/right halves.\n- Fill the quadrant containing the red control pixel with red.\n\nKey insight:\n- The red control pixel selects a whole half-grid quadrant by its row half and column half; all other foreground pixels are noise.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4a21e3da\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nCut a sprite along border-control rays and slide pieces to grid corners.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, fg (main object, ~centered), ctrl (1-2 pixels on grid edges).\n\nRule:\n- Draw rays inward from each ctrl pixel along its row/col, painting only bg cells up to the farthest fg pixel (intermittent).\n- Rays partition the grid; fg on the cut line stays in place.\n- Cut pieces in regions touching a control edge slide to that corner; others vanish.\n\nKey insight: Only regions sharing a border edge with a control pixel keep their piece; the ray skips fg cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"5961cc34\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nBuild a red ray network by activating blue blobs via directional green ports.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Large blue blobs with adjacent green pixels marking port directions.\n- Start marker: yellow pixel atop a short red segment at grid bottom.\n\nRule:\n- Extend a ray upward from start; on hitting a blue blob, recolor it red.\n- Activated blobs emit thick rays through green ports until boundary or another blob.\n- Cascade until no new blobs are reached.\n\nKey insight: Green pixels encode ray direction and width; the network activates as a chain reaction from one start point.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b9e38dc0\" lines=\"7-18\">\n<description>\nFill the region enclosed by a wall sprite and radiate color outward through its opening.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall = most frequent non-bg color, forming a partial enclosure (e.g. U-shape).\n- A fill-color pixel sits inside the enclosed area. Other non-bg pixels may act as obstacles.\nRule:\n- Flood-fill the enclosed area with the fill color, radiating outward through the wall's open end.\n- Obstacles cast \"shadows\" along the radiate direction, blocking fill behind them.\n\nKey insight: The wall endpoints define the opening direction; fill radiates outward through it, and obstacles block propagation along that same axis.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a416fc5b\" lines=\"95-106\">\n<description>\nAdd the two missing colored arms of a centered diamond-sprite motif, or clear a completed motif to a blank square.\n\nInput structure\nAn 11x11 board has orange background and pink separator bars forming nine 3x3 cells. Occupied cells contain a four-pixel diamond sprite; the center cell and one neighboring cell are red, and a completed board may also contain one grey and one teal sprite.\n\nRule\nWith only the two red sprites present, preserve the board and add grey and teal diamond sprites 135 degrees clockwise and counterclockwise from the non-center red arm. If those expected grey and teal sprites are already present, output an all-background square whose side length is the total number of sprite pixels.\n\nKey insight\nThe non-center red sprite is an eight-neighbor direction from the center; the other two colored arms are fixed rotations of that direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6ffe8f07\" lines=\"49-64\">\n<description>\nCast yellow horizontal and vertical rays from the teal rectangle until blue blockers stop them.\n\nInput structure:\n- A black-background grid containing one solid teal rectangle.\n- Blue objects are blockers; other non-background objects may lie in the ray paths.\n- Yellow is absent from the input and is the output paint color.\n\nRule:\n- From every row of the teal rectangle, cast rays left and right to the grid edge.\n- From every column of the teal rectangle, cast rays up and down to the grid edge.\n- A ray stops before a blue pixel; all crossed non-blue, non-teal pixels become yellow.\n\nKey insight:\nThe teal rectangle defines a full cross-shaped projection, while blue objects are walls and red/background cells are simply paintable space.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e4941b18\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nMove red into teal's top marker position, then move teal to the bottom outside corner of the grey rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background surrounds one filled rectangular non-background block.\n- There is exactly one red marker and one teal marker, both in the row immediately above the rectangle.\n- Both markers are horizontally aligned over columns inside the rectangle.\n\nRule\n- Clear the original red marker.\n- Recolor the original teal marker cell red.\n- Place the teal marker beside the rectangle's bottom row, on the side indicated by the horizontal direction from red to teal.\n\nKey insight\n- The red-to-teal horizontal order chooses the rectangle side where teal exits at the bottom.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"edcc2ff0\" lines=\"47-63\">\n<description>\nKeep only the lower-panel colors named by the top markers and expand each marker into an object-count bar.\n\nInput structure\nA black instruction strip sits above a non-black rectangular scene panel.  The strip contains one-cell markers\nin the first column, and each marker color is a candidate object color in the lower panel.  The lower panel has\na unique dominant background color and scattered 4-connected foreground objects.\n\nRule\nIn the lower panel, preserve objects whose colors appear as top markers and recolor every other foreground\nobject to the panel background.  In the instruction strip, replace each marker by a horizontal run from column 0\nwhose length is the number of 4-connected lower-panel objects of that marker color; erase markers with count 0.\n\nKey insight\nThe top strip is a color filter plus a compact count summary: marker colors select which lower-panel objects\nsurvive, and their bars report the number of surviving objects by color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"38007db0\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nExtract the unique cell from each lattice row into a single-column lattice.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into equal square cells by uniform gridlines of color L.\n- Cell interiors filled with bg; each cell has an inner sprite pattern.\n- Each row of cells has exactly one cell with a unique pattern; the rest are copies.\n\nRule:\n- For each lattice row, find the cell whose inner pattern appears exactly once (the \"odd one out\").\n- Construct a single-column lattice preserving the original row heights, containing only the unique cells.\n\nKey insight: The task is per-row \"odd one out\" — every cell in a row is identical except one oddball, which must be extracted.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6bcdb01e\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nExtend the edge-touching green start bar through the corridor, turning at each teal blocker until the path leaves the grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- Exactly two foreground colors are present: a 2-pixel 4-connected start bar and many blocker pixels.\n- Exactly one pixel of the start bar lies on the grid border.\n\nRule:\n- Start from the border pixel of the bar and move through the other bar pixel into the grid.\n- Paint every traversed non-blocker pixel with the start color.\n- When the next cell is a blocker, turn to the unique perpendicular non-blocker neighbor; when the next cell is outside the grid, stop.\n\nKey insight: The blocker pixels define a one-cell-wide corridor, so every interior collision has exactly one valid 90-degree turn.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"65b59efc\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDecode a stamp map: read templates, markers, and palette from a tiled grid, then render stamps.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into K tile columns (each N wide) separated by 1-col dashed separators.\n- Top band (rows 0..N-1): K template masks, one per tile column.\n- Middle band (rows N+1..2N): marker pixels whose color picks a template.\n- Bottom band (rows 2N+2..): K palette swatches giving the output color per template.\n- Separator rows at rows N and 2N+1 (dashed pattern of sep color).\n\nRule:\n- Each marker's local position (lr, lc) within its tile column determines which N×N output block to stamp.\n- The marker's color identifies which template to use; the palette recolors the template.\n\nKey insight: Marker color encodes template index; local position within tile column encodes stamp block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"edb79dae\" lines=\"39-53\">\n<description>\nStamp recolored sprites into a framed region using placeholder positions and a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background, a rectangular single-color frame, colored placeholder cells inside the frame.\n- Outside the frame: sprite shapes (one per placeholder color) and a small legend mapping each placeholder color to an output color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the frame and its interior placeholders.\n- For each placeholder color, locate the matching-color sprite outside the frame.\n- Stamp each sprite at the placeholder's position inside the frame.\n- Recolor every stamped sprite to the output color defined by the legend.\n\nKey insight: The legend pairs (overlapping two-color glyphs outside the frame) dictate the final recoloring — placeholder color selects the shape, legend maps it to the output color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"aa4ec2a5\" lines=\"52-66\">\n<description>\nAdd red outlines around objects, and mark enclosed holes inside hollow objects.\n\nInput structure:\n- A mostly uniform background contains one or more 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Some objects may enclose 4-connected background holes inside their bbox.\n\nRule:\n- For every foreground component, draw a one-cell 8-neighborhood outline in red (2).\n- If a component has enclosed background holes, recolor the component to teal (8).\n- Fill those enclosed holes with magenta (6) before drawing the outline.\n\nKey insight: Holes are treated as part of the object for outlining, while only objects\nthat actually enclose holes get the teal/magenta interior recoloring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc10701f\" lines=\"20-35\">\n<description>\nMove the orange rectangle onto the maroon rectangle and mark gated corridor crossings in red.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background contains black obstacle segments.\n- There is one solid orange rectangle and one solid maroon rectangle of the same size.\n- The two rectangles share either their row span or their column span, so the move is straight and axis-aligned.\n\nRule\n- Erase the original orange rectangle to the background.\n- Replace the maroon rectangle with the orange rectangle.\n- Between the two rectangles, paint red across the moving rectangle's width or height only at corridor cells flanked by black obstacle segments perpendicular to the movement.\n\nKey insight\n- The black segments define gates in the straight corridor; only those gated openings, not every swept background cell, become red.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"15\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"825aa9e9\" lines=\"111-125\">\n<description>\nDrop the moving-color objects downward until they rest above a one-row cushion before the obstacle or bottom edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has exactly three colors: empty background, a stationary obstacle/floor color, and a moving object color.\n- The obstacle color is the unique non-background role that touches the bottom edge.\n- Moving pixels start outside the obstacle cells, outside the row directly above each obstacle pixel, and outside the bottom row.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the background and obstacle pixels fixed.\n- Treat obstacle pixels, cells directly above obstacle pixels, and the bottom row as blocked.\n- Repeatedly move every 4-connected moving-color object one row down when its translated shape does not hit blocked cells or another moving object.\n\nKey insight: The visible floor has an invisible one-row cushion above it, so the objects fall under gravity but stop with one empty row before the floor or the grid bottom.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"16\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fe45cba4\" lines=\"72-89\">\n<description>\nMove the left-edge fragment into its same-colored anchor fragment to complete a solid rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- Exactly one foreground color is split into two 4-connected objects: one\n  touches the left edge, and the other is an anchor away from the left edge.\n- The translated left-edge object and the anchor can form one filled rectangle.\nRule\n- Find the unique split color whose left-edge object can be translated onto\n  background cells beside the other same-colored object so their union is a\n  solid axis-aligned rectangle.\n- Clear the left-edge object's original cells to the background.\n- Paint that object at the unique completing offset, preserving all other cells.\nKey insight\n- The active color is identified by object structure and rectangle completion,\n  not by a fixed color id; the same-colored anchor object stays in place.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"17\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"33067df9\" lines=\"32-48\">\n<description>\nExpand the sparse dot lattice into a 26x26 drawing with thick bars between adjacent matching dots.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground pixels lie on logical rows and columns separated by background spacer rows/columns.\n- A logical row or column is present when it contains at least one foreground pixel.\n\nRule\n- Expand every background spacer row/column to thickness 2.\n- Expand the logical rows/columns equally so the full output is 26x26.\n- Paint each foreground logical pixel as a solid rectangle of its color.\n- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels horizontally through the intervening spacer.\n- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels vertically only when neither endpoint is part of a horizontal same-color run.\n\nKey insight\n- The input is a sampled line drawing: horizontal same-color runs define the bars first, and only unclaimed same-color vertical neighbors are joined.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"18\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b0039139\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
